P2: a solo merge discards an open guard.requires, not just a missing acceptance.

At M, warrant/gate.py:1048-1060 reports both missing acceptance and unmet prerequisites
as NO_ACCEPT. _solo_window (921-935) and _watched (951-990) treat NO_ACCEPT as
waivable. _review (908-909) calls _solo, which removes that reason (912-918).
Thus a head whose guard is PENDING solely because its required obligation is open
is merge eligible, even with the review quorum already satisfied (short=0).
This contradicts docs/V3.md section 6 and section 14's solo rule: only the missing
acceptance is waived, not an unclosed requirement. The engine's guard is correct;
the gate discards its refusal. The case uses authentic deterministic fixture
signatures, an open obligation already on the base, a pr/acceptor author, a legal
stronger head guard, an add-only tail of that PR's head versions and acceptances,
and green checks. No malformed input, forged role, or unrelated tail is needed.

With this checkout's real merge.json, renamed fixture actors with the same two
pr/acceptor roles, all six combinations of ordinary/watched/constitutional and
owner present/away are eligible while the head guard is PENDING, open nonempty,
and short=0. For the two tier tests only code.py is added to the tested tier's
paths so the fixture exercises it; away uses 169 hours since green checks,
present uses 1 second. The ordinary tests use the actual policy unchanged.

Controls: no solo blocks the open prerequisite; closing it permits the accepted
head. As an unsigned diagnostic only, adding a separate OPEN_REQUIREMENT refusal
when v['open'] is nonempty in _verdict on an isolated copy makes every regression
case pass. No fix was applied to the review worktree. A repair must preserve the
requirement refusal through every solo route, with a regression and killing mutant
in an ordinary constitutional PR; the actual repair is outside this verdict.

